\f0\b\fs48 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Programming Specification for Helm\'92s Adaptive Training Engine: Architecture, Session Composition, and Autoregulation Schema\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 1. Executive Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The fundamental architectural flaw in Helm\'92s legacy training engine stems from an oversimplified volume allocation model within the 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PrescriptionEngine
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 . While weekly hard-set calculations managed by 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PlanKit
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  accurately compute volume landmarks such as Minimum Effective Volume (MEV) and Maximum Recoverable Volume (MRV), collapsing a muscle group\'92s total weekly volume into a single selected exercise per session creates severely compromised training programs. For example, when a Pull session prescribes eight weekly sets of back work and six weekly sets of bicep work across two weekly exposures, the legacy engine assigns four sets to a single back exercise (e.g., Lat Pulldown) and three sets to a single bicep movement (e.g., Standing Barbell Curl).\
This approach breaks down in real-world personal training environments due to four major physiological and biomechanical factors:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Intra-Session Stimulus Decay
\f1\b0 : Concentrating four to six consecutive hard sets onto a single movement vector produces acute localized fatigue that dramatically degrades the stimulus-to-fatigue ratio (SFR) of subsequent sets.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Anatomical and Sub-Division Neglect
\f1\b0 : Complex skeletal muscle structures\'97such as the latissimus dorsi, trapezius, and deltoids\'97comprise distinct fiber orientations and regional innervation patterns that require varied movement vectors (e.g., vertical pulling, horizontal rowing, and isolation extension) to achieve comprehensive muscular development.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Connective Tissue Micro-Trauma
\f1\b0 : Repeatedly loading a single joint angle under high volume increases cumulative mechanical stress on periarticular structures, elevating overuse injury risk compared to distributing volume across complementary movement patterns.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Session Density and Time Constraints
\f1\b0 : Single-exercise set dumping leads to unrealistic session flow and density, failing to reflect professional personal training standards.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec2 To eliminate this systemic defect without compromising offline deterministic calculation, Helm must transition from a 
\f2\i Muscle-First Set Allocator
\f1\i0  to a dual-layer 
\f0\b Movement Pattern Session Composer (MPSC)
\f1\b0  coupled with a 
\f0\b Dynamic Volume Allocation & Autoregulation System (DVAAS)
\f1\b0 . Under this updated architecture, weekly hard-set targets calculated by 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PlanKit
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  are dynamically mapped across mandatory movement pattern slots structured by session duration budgets, equipment constraints, and movement selection preferences.\

